  "finding": "The proved 54-AND multiply is a real circuit: 54 ANDs and 66 Toffolis per controlled call against the built-in 75, correct on every basis state tried (0 value mismatches, operands restored, workspace clean, phase untouched). Swapped into the six-lever arm it takes 1,325,230.0 to 1,230,640.0 Toffoli per shot: 4.652x against 4.320x, a 7.14% saving and 94,590.0 Toffolis per shot. Two corrections come with it. First, the projected 1,161,791.3 does not reproduce, and that is a modelling error rather than a shortfall in the circuit: every controlled multiply pays 12 Toffolis to add the product into its target under the control, and that part does not shrink with the AND count, so the call costs (54+12)/(63+12) rather than 54/63. Second, and larger: the multiply is 59.48% of this arm, not the 86.33% that record carries. That earlier share came from deleting `ixor_gf2_mul` and differencing the executed count, which changes the register state and therefore the predicates that gate the rest of the EEA -- the same state-perturbation the static-count rule warns about, in an executed count. Here the two arms are identical except for the multiply, so their CCX difference is exactly `calls * 9`: 10,510 executed calls, 788,250.0 Toffolis of multiply, 536,980.0 of everything else, and the reconstruction reproduces the measured arm to +0.000000. That decomposition also prices the rest of the ladder: at 54 ANDs the arm is 4.652x, at 23 ANDs 6.327x (2.03x the 445,229 target), and with a free multiply 8.634x (1.49x the target) -- so the earlier conclusion holds with a narrower margin: no multiply reaches Benchmark II on this decoder."
